# Introduction

# Actuarial nearest age

Hospitals, schools, insurers, and pension systems all compute ages, and each has its own rule for leap-day birthdays, month-end anniversaries, and rounding. The rules sound simple until a February 29 birthday meets a common year, or a due date lands on a month with fewer days.

These exercises are about applying one stated age policy exactly, even at the awkward boundaries.

# Instructions

# Instructions

Implement `std::optional<std::int64_t> temporal_actuarial(ActuarialBirth birth, Date as_of)` in `temporal-actuarial.h` with exactly the documented return type.

Round age to the nearer birthday using exact calendar-day distance. Exact midpoint ties round down. The return rounds completed age upward only when the next birthday is strictly closer. Invalid or reversed dates are rejected, and all year arithmetic is checked.

## Examples

```cpp
auto a=temporal_actuarial(ActuarialBirth{2000,1,1},Date{2020,7,3}); require(a&&*a==21); auto b=temporal_actuarial(ActuarialBirth{2000,1,1},Date{2020,6,30}); require(b&&*b==20);
```

In particular, using six-month rule does not satisfy the rules above. All
arithmetic must be defined C++17 integer arithmetic; do not use wall-clock
state, floating point, a platform date library, or hard-coded answers.
Complexity is linear in supplied sequences and constant otherwise.

