# Introduction

# Checked affine clock map

Timestamp arithmetic overflows quietly: add one second too many and a 64-bit counter wraps into a nonsense value. Defensive systems never let that happen; every addition, multiplication, and scaling step is checked before it is evaluated.

These exercises are about composing time arithmetic that reports failure cleanly instead of wrapping.

# Instructions

# Instructions

Implement `std::optional<std::int64_t> temporal_affine_map(std::int64_t sample, std::int64_t origin, std::int64_t numerator, std::int64_t denominator)` in `temporal-affine-map.h` with exactly the documented return type.

Map a timestamp through a rational affine clock relation. Reduce factors, round nearest-even, and reject every overflow. The return is a checked nearest-even rational affine mapping around origin. A nonpositive denominator or any intermediate overflow is rejected.

## Examples

```cpp
auto a=temporal_affine_map(14,10,3,2); require(a&&*a==16);
```

In particular, using double arithmetic does not satisfy the rules above. All
arithmetic must be defined C++17 integer arithmetic; do not use wall-clock
state, floating point, a platform date library, or hard-coded answers.
Complexity is linear in supplied sequences and constant otherwise.

