# Introduction

# Clamped calendar age

Hospitals, schools, insurers, and pension systems all compute ages, and each has its own rule for leap-day birthdays, month-end anniversaries, and rounding. The rules sound simple until a February 29 birthday meets a common year, or a due date lands on a month with fewer days.

These exercises are about applying one stated age policy exactly, even at the awkward boundaries.

# Instructions

# Instructions

Implement `std::optional<AgeParts> temporal_age_clamped(Date birth, Date as_of)` in `temporal-age-clamped.h` with exactly the documented return type.

Return canonical year/month/day age by clamping invalid monthly anniversaries. Components reconstruct the reference date from the birth date under the clamp policy. AgeParts applies clamped anniversaries, then clamped whole months, then exact residual days. Invalid or reversed dates are rejected.

## Examples

```cpp
auto a=temporal_age_clamped(Date{2020,1,31},Date{2021,3,3}); require(a && a->years==1 && a->months==1 && a->days==3);
```

In particular, using fixed 30-day borrow does not satisfy the rules above. All
arithmetic must be defined C++17 integer arithmetic; do not use wall-clock
state, floating point, a platform date library, or hard-coded answers.
Complexity is linear in supplied sequences and constant otherwise.

