# Introduction

# Event age series

Hospitals, schools, insurers, and pension systems all compute ages, and each has its own rule for leap-day birthdays, month-end anniversaries, and rounding. The rules sound simple until a February 29 birthday meets a common year, or a due date lands on a month with fewer days.

These exercises are about applying one stated age policy exactly, even at the awkward boundaries.

# Instructions

# Instructions

Implement `std::optional<std::vector<std::int64_t>> temporal_age_series(Date birth, const std::vector<Date>& events)` in `temporal-age-series.h` with exactly the documented return type.

Compute completed age for a nondecreasing sequence of event dates. Reject disorder transactionally and advance only at anniversaries. The vector contains transactional completed ages for nondecreasing valid events. Disordered, invalid, or pre-birth events and unrepresentable year differences are rejected.

## Examples

```cpp
auto a=temporal_age_series(Date{2000,12,31},{Date{2019,12,30},Date{2019,12,31},Date{2020,1,1}}); require(a&&*a==std::vector<std::int64_t>({18,19,19}));
```

In particular, using year subtraction does not satisfy the rules above. All
arithmetic must be defined C++17 integer arithmetic; do not use wall-clock
state, floating point, a platform date library, or hard-coded answers.
Complexity is linear in supplied sequences and constant otherwise.

