# Introduction

# Temporal shard key

Counters wrap. A 16-bit timer rolls over every few minutes, serial numbers repeat, and streams from many sensors arrive carrying only modular labels. Ordering and unwrapping such observations is daily work in embedded and network code.

These exercises are about reconstructing a sensible absolute timeline from wrapped or out-of-order evidence.

# Instructions

# Instructions

Implement `std::optional<ShardKey> temporal_euclidean_shard_key(std::int64_t instant, std::int64_t anchor, std::uint64_t width, std::uint32_t shard_count)` in `temporal-euclidean-shard-key.h` with exactly the documented return type.

Compute Euclidean time bucket, nonnegative shard, and checked bucket origin. Reject zero shard count and avoid negative C++ remainders. ShardKey combines Euclidean bucket, nonnegative shard, and checked bucket origin. Zero width, zero shard count, or overflowing arithmetic is rejected.

## Examples

```cpp
auto a=temporal_euclidean_shard_key(-1,0,10,4); require(a&&a->bucket==-1&&a->shard==3&&a->origin==-10);
```

In particular, using raw remainder does not satisfy the rules above. All
arithmetic must be defined C++17 integer arithmetic; do not use wall-clock
state, floating point, a platform date library, or hard-coded answers.
Complexity is linear in supplied sequences and constant otherwise.

