# Introduction

# Completed ISO-week age

Hospitals, schools, insurers, and pension systems all compute ages, and each has its own rule for leap-day birthdays, month-end anniversaries, and rounding. The rules sound simple until a February 29 birthday meets a common year, or a due date lands on a month with fewer days.

These exercises are about applying one stated age policy exactly, even at the awkward boundaries.

# Instructions

# Instructions

Implement `std::optional<std::int64_t> temporal_iso_weeks(Date start, Date finish)` in `temporal-iso-weeks.h` with exactly the documented return type.

Count complete seven-day periods between valid dates. Use exact Gregorian day difference across ISO year boundaries. The return is exact complete seven-day periods across Gregorian/ISO-year boundaries. Invalid or reversed dates are rejected.

## Examples

```cpp
auto a=temporal_iso_weeks(Date{2020,12,28},Date{2021,1,11}); require(a&&*a==2);
```

In particular, using week-label subtraction does not satisfy the rules above. All
arithmetic must be defined C++17 integer arithmetic; do not use wall-clock
state, floating point, a platform date library, or hard-coded answers.
Complexity is linear in supplied sequences and constant otherwise.

