# Introduction

# Checked second/nanosecond join

Timestamp arithmetic overflows quietly: add one second too many and a 64-bit counter wraps into a nonsense value. Defensive systems never let that happen; every addition, multiplication, and scaling step is checked before it is evaluated.

These exercises are about composing time arithmetic that reports failure cleanly instead of wrapping.

# Instructions

# Instructions

Implement `std::optional<std::int64_t> temporal_join_nanos(std::int64_t seconds, std::int32_t nanos)` in `temporal-join-nanos.h` with exactly the documented return type.

Join canonical seconds and nanoseconds into one signed nanosecond count. Nanoseconds must already be in [0, 1e9). The return joins already-canonical nanoseconds; normalization is deliberately forbidden.

## Examples

