# Introduction

# Transactional duration ledger

Timestamp arithmetic overflows quietly: add one second too many and a 64-bit counter wraps into a nonsense value. Defensive systems never let that happen; every addition, multiplication, and scaling step is checked before it is evaluated.

These exercises are about composing time arithmetic that reports failure cleanly instead of wrapping.

# Instructions

# Instructions

Implement `bool temporal_transactional_sum(std::int64_t start, const std::vector<std::int64_t>& deltas, std::int64_t& output)` in `temporal-transactional-sum.h` with exactly the documented return type.

Apply signed deltas in order and commit only when every prefix is representable. Late failure leaves output unchanged. True commits the final prefix sum; false leaves caller-owned output unchanged.

## Examples

```cpp
std::int64_t out=9; require(temporal_transactional_sum(1,{2,3},out)&&out==6);
```

In particular, using partial commit does not satisfy the rules above. All
arithmetic must be defined C++17 integer arithmetic; do not use wall-clock
state, floating point, a platform date library, or hard-coded answers.
Complexity is linear in supplied sequences and constant otherwise.

