# Introduction

# Weighted timestamp centroid

Timestamp arithmetic overflows quietly: add one second too many and a 64-bit counter wraps into a nonsense value. Defensive systems never let that happen; every addition, multiplication, and scaling step is checked before it is evaluated.

These exercises are about composing time arithmetic that reports failure cleanly instead of wrapping.

# Instructions

# Instructions

Implement `std::optional<std::int64_t> temporal_weighted_centroid(const std::vector<WeightedStamp>& samples, std::int64_t pivot)` in `temporal-weighted-centroid.h` with exactly the documented return type.

Compute a positive-weight timestamp centroid around a pivot. Use checked weighted deltas and nearest-even rounding. The return is a pivoted checked positive-weight centroid rounded nearest-even. Empty samples, nonpositive weights, or overflow are rejected.

## Examples

```cpp
auto a=temporal_weighted_centroid({{10,1},{20,3}},10); require(a&&*a==18);
```

In particular, using absolute products does not satisfy the rules above. All
arithmetic must be defined C++17 integer arithmetic; do not use wall-clock
state, floating point, a platform date library, or hard-coded answers.
Complexity is linear in supplied sequences and constant otherwise.

