# Introduction

# Checked window count

Timestamp arithmetic overflows quietly: add one second too many and a 64-bit counter wraps into a nonsense value. Defensive systems never let that happen; every addition, multiplication, and scaling step is checked before it is evaluated.

These exercises are about composing time arithmetic that reports failure cleanly instead of wrapping.

# Instructions

# Instructions

Implement `std::optional<std::uint64_t> temporal_window_count(std::int64_t begin, std::int64_t end, std::uint64_t width)` in `temporal-window-count.h` with exactly the documented return type.

Count aligned half-open windows between signed endpoints. Avoid signed end-start overflow and reject zero width or reversed endpoints. The return is the ceiling number of aligned half-open windows, using unsigned distance to avoid subtraction overflow.

## Examples

```cpp
auto a=temporal_window_count(-5,6,4); require(a&&*a==3);
```

In particular, using signed subtraction does not satisfy the rules above. All
arithmetic must be defined C++17 integer arithmetic; do not use wall-clock
state, floating point, a platform date library, or hard-coded answers.
Complexity is linear in supplied sequences and constant otherwise.

